welcome to this video about algebraic  restriction codes and their applications  my name is cesco and this is joint work  with divish nico mohammad giulio and  machi  first i'm going to introduce algebraic  restriction codes then i will show you  how we use them inside of cryptography  and finally and we're going to show you  a construction of an algebraic  restriction code  imagine you have a device  where on input x0 and x1  where both of these are vectors  this device is supposed to publish a  times x0 plus x1  where a is a scalar this device however  can misbehave and instead of multiplying  the scalar a  multiply an arbitrary matrix a to x0 and  publish the result and this is what we  want to stop with algebraic restriction  codes  so what we do instead is use some  randomness r to encode x0 and x1  into x0 head and x1 hat then we send  this off to the device  the device will publish  a x0 hat plus  x1 hat  now we also publish r  and the public can reconstruct  a x0 plus x1 from these two  values  if however  the device misbehaves now  and publishes  a times x0 head plus  x1 hat  the public cannot reconstruct  small a times x0  plus x1  or any other value  dependent on x0 or x1  now that was an example  now i'm going to go into the definition  of algebraic restriction codes  we have two sets of functions  f which is a subset of g  f contains all the functions that the  device is supposed to be able to  evaluate  g however contains all the functions  that the device can actually evaluate an  algebraic restriction code has two  algorithms  an encoding algorithm which uses some  randomness to encode its input x  into x hat  then a function from either of those two  sets  can be  evaluated on the encoded input  this results in an encoded output  the encoded output can then using the  same randomness as before  be  decoded again  now this is correct  if for every function in f  the decoded value is the same as if you  evaluated the function  on the original  input  however  if you use a function outside of f but  inside of g  then the output of the decoded  value  should be uniformly random  now i'm going to show you how we use  algebraic restriction codes in  cryptography namely embedding a  oblivious transfer protocol  oblivious transfer is a two-party  protocol between a receiver and a sender  the receiver contributes a choice bit b  and the sender contributes two strings  m0 and m1  and at the end of the protocol the  receiver learns  m  b  however she does not learn  m one minus b  and the sender does not learn b  oblivious transfer is  interesting as it is sufficient to build  arbitrary secure multi-party computation  protocols  using algebraic restriction codes we can  build a rate one statistically send a  private to message oblivious transfer  protocol without any setup assumptions  or just under the decisional  diffie-hellman assumption  to understand the oblivious transfer  protocol i first going to explain to you  linearly homomorphic encryption  a linearly homomorphic encryption  is a special kind of public key  encryption a public key encryption  scheme  has two keys a public key pk and a  secret key sk  the public key here is used to encrypt a  message m into a  ciphertext then  the secret key sk can be used to  retrieve the message back from the  ciphertext in the scheme i'm talking  about currently the messages are n by n  matrices  over the integers modulo p  a linearly homomorphic encryption scheme  additionally has the ability to modify  the ciphertext using the  vectors a and b such that if you modify  it  you get a new ciphertext c prime  and if you decrypt the new ciphertext c  prime using the secret key again you get  the original plaintext m multiplied by a  plus b  a and b here are the vectors from this  evaluation procedure now i'll show you  the linearly homomorphic encryption  scheme already is almost an oblivious  transfer protocol  the receiver simply multiplies its  choice bit b by an identity matrix of  size n  and then encrypts it inside of the  linearly homomorphic encryption scheme  it sends the ciphertext together with  the public key to the sender  the sender then evaluates a function  which multiplies  m1 minus m0 to b and adds m0 this is  evaluated on the ciphertext and yields a  new ciphertext c prime this new  ciphertext c prime is then sent to the  receiver who can simply decrypt it  and  retrieve mb  the main problem with this setup however  is that if the receiver wants to cheat  she can simply  encrypt an arbitrary matrix a and send  it to the sender  if the sender then goes ahead and does  the same thing as before multiplying by  m1 minus m0 and adding m 0  the receiver can learn the result of  this function which she is not supposed  to learn  this already sounds a lot like the  problem i've outlined earlier an  adversary can  multiply by an arbitrary matrix instead  of just a scalar  now we can stop this by using algebraic  restriction codes  now we simply modify the protocol by  first encoding m0 and m1 to m0 head and  m1 head  then  if the receiver encrypts the matrix  b times  identity matrix  and the evaluator  evaluates the function multiplying by m1  hat minus m0 and adding m0  the receiver learns  mb head  and can decode it using the randomness r  to mb  if however the receiver encrypts a  matrix which is not a scaled unit matrix  the receiver  will only learn  a times  m one hat minus m0 hat  plus  m0  as we know from the definition of  algebraic restriction codes  this will result in a uniformly random  value  finally i want to take a look at our  explicit constructions of algebraic  restriction codes  the first one is the one that we've  already looked at  where the  allowed set of functions simply scale x0  by  a scalar and add x1  but the possible functions are the  functions where x0 is multiplied by an  arbitrary matrix and x1 is  added you know in our second  construction  the allowed functions  take the input and multiply i